<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
Gazebo plugins + camera sensor for Scout Mini, corrected to real hardware values.
Structure copied from scout_ros2's gazebo.xacro (same DiffDrive/sensor plugin
wiring Part A already debugged - topic names, plugin types, etc. all unchanged).

Two real, confirmed corrections made here, found by comparing this file against
what the SRU policy was actually trained on:

1. DiffDrive wheel_separation/wheel_radius were Scout 2.0's (0.58306m / 0.16459m,
   from scout_v2.xacro) - now Scout Mini's real, confirmed values (0.49m / 0.08m,
   PLAN.md sec 6). Note: a wheel-size mismatch alone would NOT cause wrong-direction
   driving (DiffDrive still correctly converts a commanded (v, omega) into whatever
   wheel speeds achieve that motion, using its own configured wheel size) - but it
   does mean the robot's real-world footprint/turning behavior didn't match what was
   trained on, which matters for a fair test.

2. Camera mount was still B2W's: origin rpy="0 0.349066 0" (20 degrees pitched
   down, inherited from B2W's original static tf) and horizontal_fov=1.845264
   (~105.7 degrees, matches ZED-X, not ZED 2). Confirmed both were never updated
   for Scout Mini. Now: level (no tilt, per your explicit confirmation - "not tilt
   at any degree, it is facing the front") and ZED 2's real 110-degree horizontal
   FOV (110 * pi/180 = 1.9198621771937625 rad). Mount position (0.06m forward,
   0.257m up) matches the same real measurement already used in
   scout_mini.urdf (the IsaacLab training asset) - see that file's header for the
   derivation from the confirmed 19.2cm-above-chassis-top / 6cm-forward
   measurement.

Image resolution (240x150) and clip range (0.3-20.0m) are left unchanged - clip
range already matches ZED 2's real spec, and resolution is a simulation-efficiency
choice (raycasting resolution, not required to match the real sensor's native
pixel count) rather than something confirmed wrong.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://ros.org/wiki/xacro">

    <gazebo>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
            <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
        </plugin>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-diff-drive-system" name="gz::sim::systems::DiffDrive">
            <left_joint>front_left_wheel</left_joint>
            <left_joint>rear_left_wheel</left_joint>
            <right_joint>front_right_wheel</right_joint>
            <right_joint>rear_right_wheel</right_joint>
            <!-- wheel_separation set to 0.576 (0.49 x ~1.176), not the real 0.49m -
                 revised down from an earlier 0.686 (0.49 x 1.4). The 1.4 figure
                 came from AgileX's OLD Scout 2.0 ROS1/Gazebo-Classic controller
                 (agilexrobotics/ugv_gazebo_sim, scout_skid_steer.cpp) - a
                 different robot generation, different plugin architecture. Found
                 a more directly comparable, real source since: a community ROS2
                 Jazzy + Gazebo Harmonic port SPECIFICALLY for Scout Mini
                 (mattiadutto/ugv_gazebo_sim, scout/scout_description/urdf/
                 scout_mini.xacro - fetched and read directly), using the SAME
                 gz-sim DiffDrive plugin we use. Its own wheel_separation (0.49)
                 vs its own physical wheel origins (y=+/-0.2082515, i.e. physical
                 track=0.4165) implies a ratio of ~1.176, not 1.4. Applied to our
                 confirmed-correct physical track (0.49m): 0.49*1.176=0.576.
                 Still flagged as a starting point (from a different but far more
                 comparable source), not a confirmed Scout-Mini-specific fact.
                 DiffDrive computes wheel_speed = (v +/- omega*half_separation)/radius;
                 raising this only boosts the omega (rotation) term's sensitivity -
                 v-only (straight-line) commands are entirely unaffected, since
                 wheel_separation doesn't appear in that part of the formula.
                 Real caveat, not glossed over: since this same wheel_separation
                 value also feeds DiffDrive's own odometry computation below,
                 inflating it will also distort wheel-based /odom's own internal
                 assumption about the actuation-to-rotation relationship, on top
                 of the wheel-slip drift it already has. Given /odom is already
                 known-unreliable and ground_truth_odom exists for comparison,
                 this is an acceptable secondary imperfection, not ignored. -->
            <wheel_separation>0.576</wheel_separation>
            <wheel_radius>0.08</wheel_radius>
            <topic>cmd_vel</topic>
            <!-- tf_topic REMOVED here (was <tf_topic>tf</tf_topic>) - moved to
                 the ground_truth_odom OdometryPublisher plugin below instead.
                 Found 2026-08-19: RViz (loaded via scout_mini_nav.rviz for
                 live goal/trajectory visualization, since Gazebo's own GUI
                 can't render ROS visualization_msgs/Marker) was showing the
                 robot spinning in place while Gazebo's own view showed it
                 correctly driving toward the goal - because RViz's
                 RobotModel/TF display follows the odom->base_link TF this
                 plugin broadcasts, and that TF is DiffDrive's own
                 wheel-INTEGRATED estimate - the exact same known-unreliable
                 source already root-caused earlier (up to 225 degrees of
                 heading divergence from ground truth measured directly,
                 which is why rl_nav_controller's actual navigation decisions
                 were already switched to consume ground_truth_odom instead,
                 see rl_nav_controller.launch.py). This plugin was still the
                 sole TF broadcaster though, so any TF-based visualization
                 (RViz) was unknowingly watching the same corrupted estimate.
                 A frame can only have ONE parent in TF2's tree at a time, so
                 ground_truth_odom->base_link and odom->base_link can't both
                 broadcast simultaneously (base_link would get two competing
                 parents) - picked ground_truth_odom as the sole TF source
                 since it's what should drive any visualization meant to
                 reflect real robot behavior. /odom (this plugin's own
                 Odometry MESSAGE, not TF) is UNCHANGED and still publishes
                 for wheel-vs-ground-truth comparison purposes - only the TF
                 broadcast moved. If a future need arises to visualize wheel
                 odometry's OWN (wrong) trajectory in RViz, that needs a
                 separate ghost frame, not this plugin's TF back. -->
            <!-- Odometry publishing restored (odom_publish_frequency/odom_topic/
                 frame_id/child_frame_id) - real hardware deployment uses wheel
                 odometry (+ camera), not ground truth, so /odom needs to reflect
                 that same real error characteristic (dead-reckoning drift under
                 wheel slip) to be representative. Confirmed real: this odometry
                 IS wheel-integrated, not chassis-position-based - it will drift
                 from ground truth exactly the way real wheel odometry would
                 (measured once at 10+ meters off during a genuine stuck/wheel-
                 slip event). OdometryPublisher below now publishes ground truth
                 to a SEPARATE topic for ongoing side-by-side comparison, not as
                 the primary /odom source.
                 max_linear_acceleration/max_angular_acceleration re-added.
                 Ruled out as the cause of the under-rotation anomaly: removing
                 them (retested with an identical 6s pure-rotation ground-truth
                 test) made actual rotation SMALLER (~90 deg vs ~180 deg with the
                 limits present), the opposite of what "limits are throttling it"
                 would predict - so keeping them (real, sourced, generally-good
                 practice; see the earlier note on where these values come from).
                 Values revised DOWN from 3.0/6.0 (Husky, a different/heavier
                 robot) to 1.0/1.0, matching mattiadutto/ugv_gazebo_sim's real
                 Scout Mini Harmonic port exactly (fetched and read directly, see
                 wheel_separation comment above for the same source). Also added
                 max_wheel_torque=20 - a real parameter that source uses and we
                 were missing entirely; a hard torque ceiling independent of the
                 acceleration limit. Motivated by a real incident, not just
                 caution: a live test under the old (3.0/6.0, no torque cap)
                 config showed angular_z pinned at max clamp continuously with
                 no arrival ever detected, and the robot drove off the terrain
                 edge - exactly the failure mode tighter limits are meant to
                 prevent. Current leading hypothesis for the underlying
                 under-rotation-during-pivot phenomenon itself (separate from
                 this safety config): Scout Mini is 4-wheel skid-steer (no free-
                 castering wheels) - in-place pivoting requires all four wheels
                 to scrub sideways against friction, a real physical effect real
                 skid-steer vehicles also under-achieve the naive kinematic
                 turn-rate formula for. Run-to-run inconsistency in earlier
                 ad hoc trials was later found to be contaminated by a test-
                 methodology bug (no final zero-velocity command sent, and
                 DiffDrive has no cmd_vel watchdog, so the robot kept coasting
                 between trials) - not necessarily physics non-determinism. -->
            <odom_publish_frequency>50</odom_publish_frequency>
            <odom_topic>odom</odom_topic>
            <frame_id>odom</frame_id>
            <child_frame_id>base_link</child_frame_id>
            <max_linear_acceleration>1.0</max_linear_acceleration>
            <min_linear_acceleration>-1.0</min_linear_acceleration>
            <max_angular_acceleration>1.0</max_angular_acceleration>
            <min_angular_acceleration>-1.0</min_angular_acceleration>
            <max_wheel_torque>20</max_wheel_torque>
        </plugin>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system" name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher">
            <odom_frame>ground_truth_odom</odom_frame>
            <robot_base_frame>base_link</robot_base_frame>
            <odom_publish_frequency>50</odom_publish_frequency>
            <odom_topic>ground_truth_odom</odom_topic>
            <dimensions>2</dimensions>
            <!-- Added - this plugin is now the sole TF broadcaster
                 (ground_truth_odom -> base_link), moved from DiffDrive above.
                 Same gz topic name "tf" as before, so scout_gz_bridge.yaml's
                 existing gz "tf" -> ROS "/tf" bridge entry needs no change -
                 only WHICH plugin feeds it changed, not the bridge itself. -->
            <tf_topic>tf</tf_topic>
        </plugin>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system" name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
            <topic>joint_states</topic>
        </plugin>
    </gazebo>

    <link name="zed_x_camera">
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry><box size="0.05 0.1 0.01"/></geometry>
        </collision>
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="0.01"/>
            <inertia ixx="0.01" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="0.01" iyz="0" izz="0.01"/>
        </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="base_to_zed_x_camera" type="fixed">
        <parent link="base_link"/>
        <child link="zed_x_camera"/>
        <origin xyz="0.06 0.0 0.257" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <gazebo reference="base_to_zed_x_camera">
        <preserveFixedJoint>true</preserveFixedJoint>
    </gazebo>

    <gazebo reference="zed_x_camera">
        <sensor name="zed_x_camera" type="depth_camera">
            <always_on>1</always_on>
            <update_rate>15</update_rate>
            <visualize>true</visualize>
            <topic>zed_x_camera</topic>
            <camera>
                <!-- Was 1.9198621771937625 rad (110 deg, real ZED 2 datasheet spec) -
                     wrong target for a SIMULATION test. Training's raycast_camera
                     (scout_mini_env_cfg.py) used a pinhole intrinsic matrix with a
                     single focal length (fx=fy=26.5px at 64x40, i.e. NOT matched to
                     the real camera's published FOV) - which works out to an
                     ACTUAL rendered FOV of 100.742 deg (H) x 74.085 deg (V), not
                     110x83.5. Gazebo only exposes horizontal_fov (vertical is
                     auto-derived from this plus the image aspect ratio) - since
                     240x150 and 64x40 share the same 1.6:1 aspect ratio, this one
                     value reproduces training's exact projection geometry. Matching
                     the real ZED 2 spec (110/70) matters for eventual real-hardware
                     deployment, not for testing a sim-trained policy in sim. -->
                <horizontal_fov>1.7582794098833934</horizontal_fov>
                <image>
                    <width>240</width>
                    <height>150</height>
                    <format>R_FLOAT32</format>
                </image>
                <clip><near>0.3</near><far>20.0</far></clip>
                <optical_frame_id>zed_x_camera</optical_frame_id>
                <depth_camera><output>depths</output></depth_camera>
            </camera>
        </sensor>
    </gazebo>

</robot>
